    id: "zone-2-why", title: "Why so much Zone 2", topic: "training", grade: "A",
    tags: ["zone 2", "easy", "slow", "boring", "aerobic base", "why easy", "mitochondria", "fat oxidation", "base phase", "conversational"],
    body: "Zone 2 (below the first lactate threshold, able to speak in sentences) is where the aerobic engine is built: more mitochondria and capillaries in slow-twitch fibres, higher fat oxidation at a given pace, and a lower heart rate at race pace. It is also the intensity that can be repeated day after day without accumulating damage, which is what allows the volume a long-course race needs. Going faster on easy days produces more fatigue for the same aerobic gain and takes the edge off the hard days. In base, about 85 % of time is Zone 1–2; in build, about 75 %.",
